    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-75155",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)(3+0)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
